  "ref": "ECC.9.2",
  "review_notes": [
    "The compound particle of the opening clause takes *come alike* as one unit. *come* is the linking verb this verbless clause supplies where a copula would stand, and under rule 20 it joins the predicate rather than standing `added`, exactly as *seemed* joins the predicate adjective at ECC.9.13; rule 30 states that a copula the construction hands to the whole of an expression does not defeat its one-rendering condition. Reconciled at the chapter pass; the competing reading, which the verse pass took, marks *come* a wholly supplied lexical verb under rule 20's supplied-lexical-verb clause.",
    "The second compound particle (s046 with s047) is split instead, *as* to the preposition and *he that* to the relative, because each morpheme has an English word of its own there (rule 2); only the first meets rule 30's condition.",
    "Both relative phrases rendered *him that* and *he that* follow rule 46's first rider, the present relative taking the pronoun and *that* rather than the antecedent being supplied to the participle.",
    "The italic *and* before *he that sweareth* is `added`: the clause writes no conjunction (rule 14).",
    "The waw before *to him that sacrificeth* is the surplus one: four written *and*s are set against five waws by the members each stands between (rule 42's counting)."
  ]
}
